Using spot help in LispView

LispView has limited support for spot help. It is intentionally not
exported.  XView spot help for widgets in OpenWindows version 2.0 assumes
that the widgets panel contains a visible mapped text-field (in order to
obtain the keyboard focus).  If you want to experiment with spot help, you
will need to do the following:

- Create one or more help files: specially formatted files with a
".info" extension.  The help files are organized as named records, e.g.:

:NoHelpThere
This isn't too useful.

:HelpHere
Various helpful remarks...
More helpful remarks...

The names of the records are the single words with a ":" prefix, the
contents of the record is everything up to the next record name or EOF.


- Set the HELPPATH Unix environment variable to include the
directory that contains your help files.  For example if you have
a help file called "frob-help.info" in directory /home/foo/baz
then update the environment-variable like this:

(setf (LCL:environment-variable "HELPPATH") 
      (format nil "/home/foo/baz/:~A" (LCL:environment-variable "HELPPATH")))

- Export the LispView spot-help accessor as follows:

(LV::def-solo-accessor LV:SPOT-HELP LV:item :type string
					    :driver LV::dd-item-spot-help)


- Associate one record from a ".info" file with a panel item with the 
spot-help accessor:

(setf w (make-instance 'lv:base-window)
      p (make-instance 'lv:panel :parent w)
      b (make-instance 'lv:command-button 
	  :parent p 
	  :label "I'm Helpful") 
      f (make-instance 'lv:text-field 
	  :parent p))

(setf (lv:spot-help f) "frob-help:HelpHere")
(setf (lv:spot-help b) "frob-help:NoHelpThere")

The value of spot-help is a string: "<help-file><record-name>", 
<help-file> is the name of the help file without an extension, 
<record-name> is the name of the record - which must begin with a ":".

(Note: The :spot-help initarg does not work properly.)


- Before the Help key will work you'll need to make sure that the text-field
has the keyboard focus (you may have to press the mouse button on it) .


Changes you make to a help file after it's been used to bring up a spot
help popup window may not have any effect -- XView caches the contents of
the help file.

